Hi Patrio Gabriel,

Coding agents are the fastest-growing use case on OpenRouter. One API key g=
ives your agent access to every model, with automatic provider fallback if =
one goes down. You stop maintaining four provider accounts and one consolid=
ated usage view replaces four separate dashboards.

See integrations ( https://openrouter.ai/docs/frameworks?utm_source=3Demail=
&utm_medium=3Dlifecycle&utm_campaign=3Donboarding_v2&utm_content=3Db9_agent=
s&utm_campaign=3DOnboarding+Flow+-+v4+April+2026&utm_content=3DA6+Coding+Ag=
ents&utm_medium=3Demail_action&utm_source=3Dcustomer.io )

=E2=80=83

The major agents already speak OpenRouter.

Cursor. Drop your OpenRouter key into Settings and pick any model from the =
dropdown. Switch between Claude, GPT, Gemini, and DeepSeek per task without=
 leaving the editor.
Cline. Autonomous coding with auto-routing. The agent picks the right model=
 per step, so planning runs on a thinking model and execution runs on somet=
hing faster and cheaper.
Continue. Open-source, configurable. Point the base URL at OpenRouter and e=
very model is available from the model picker.
Claude Code. Use OpenRouter as the backend to track team usage, set per-key=
 rate limits, and control spend across models without touching Anthropic bi=
lling directly.

Why the multi-model swap matters for agents. Most coding agents are cheaper=
 and faster when you route structured steps like file reads and diff writes=
 to a small model, and reserve a frontier model for the part that actually =
writes code. The cost gap between a $0.15/M token small model and a $15/M t=
oken frontier model stacks up fast when the agent makes 50 calls per task.

Usage visibility. The activity dashboard shows every request with its model=
, cost, latency, and token counts. If an agent runs away, you see it inside=
 minutes, not at the end of a billing cycle. Per-key rate limits stop the b=
leeding without disabling the whole integration.

